       ONE thing that scrolls, matching desktop's .window/.window-content
       split exactly. Since the window's own edge now never grows past
       where MobileDock sits, the old padding-bottom:84px dock-clearance
       reservation is gone too — there's nothing left for it to protect
       against, the boundary itself makes the collision impossible.

       Margin sized off MobileDock's own real footprint (padding 10px top
       + 44px icon + 11px bottom = 65px tall, floating
       max(20px, safe-area+12px) off the viewport bottom — see
       MobileDock.astro) plus a real gap on top of that, so the window's
       own bottom edge visibly clears the dock rather than running behind
       it.

       transition: only border-radius/box-shadow here — the actual
       size/position change (height, margin) is handled by a proper FLIP
       animation instead (see flipMobileWindow in MobileTitlebar.astro),
       not a plain CSS transition on height/margin directly. Those ARE
       technically animatable properties, but user feedback after that
       first attempt was "no animation still... no scaling animation of
       window nothing" — border-radius/box-shadow transitioning smoothly
       while the actual resize snapped suggests something about animating
       layout-shifting properties (height/margin, which trigger reflow
       every frame) directly wasn't working reliably, where a transform
       (GPU-composited, the same technique desktop's own maximize already
       uses via Window.astro's flipResize) is the proven-reliable
       approach in this codebase for exactly this kind of "instant CSS
       state change that needs to look like a smooth resize." */
    /* Base rule is deliberately bare — NO background/blur/radius/shadow/
       side-margin here. Those are the "floating window" chrome, which only
       makes sense for pages that actually render a MobileTitlebar (a
       window's traffic lights) — see the :has(.m-titlebar) rule below,
       which is the ONLY place that chrome gets added. Home has no
       titlebar (matches desktop, where Home is the desktop itself, not a
       window sitting on it) and was wrongly getting the same box/blur
       treatment anyway, since this rule used to apply it unconditionally —
       found live from direct feedback ("why is there a bounding box on
       home page? that is only needed for windows"). Height/overflow/flex
       stay unscoped though: every mobile page still needs the screen
       itself to never scroll (only its own last child scrolls, see
       below) — that requirement isn't specific to windows, just applied
       via a shorter, chrome-free box for Home. */
    .mobile-content {
      position: relative;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      overflow: hidden;
      box-sizing: border-box;
      height: 100dvh;
      padding-top: env(safe-area-inset-top, 0px);
    }

    /* :global() required — the actual last-child element (.m-work,
       .m-music, .m-home, ...) belongs to whichever PAGE file renders it,
       not this one, same cross-file-slot scoping trap noted elsewhere in
       this project (see MEMORY.md's Astro gotchas entry) — a plain scoped
       rule here would silently match nothing. .m-home-backdrop (Home's
       own decorative layer, when present) is position:fixed and so
       doesn't participate in flex layout/DOM-order sizing the normal way,
       leaving the REAL content wrapper as the effective last flex child
       either way, titlebar-bearing pages or not. */
    :global(.mobile-content > *:last-child) {
      flex: 1 0 0;
      min-height: 0;
      overflow-y: auto;
      scrollbar-width: none;
      padding-bottom: 32px;
    }

    :global(.mobile-content > *:last-child::-webkit-scrollbar) {
      display: none;
    }

    /* Real bug caught live: every one of these page wrappers (.m-work,
       .m-reading, etc) is ITSELF a flex column for its own children
       (heading, grid, tab switcher, panels...), and a flex item's default
       flex-shrink:1 doesn't care that its parent has overflow-y:auto —
       once the parent's own height is bounded (it now is, see above), the
       flex algorithm shrinks children to fit that bound REGARDLESS of
       overflow, squishing them instead of letting the parent scroll past
       them. Confirmed live on reading.astro: .m-reading-switcher (the
       Books/Movies pill toggle) computed to height:0px, fully collapsed
       and invisible, while still present in the DOM — this is what read
       as content being "literally removed." flex-shrink:0 on every DIRECT
       child of the scrollable wrapper (not just the switcher/panels on
       tabbed pages — project-card grids, headings, everything) stops the
       shrink entirely, so content keeps its natural size and the
       overflow-y:auto on the wrapper itself is what actually handles
       anything taller than the window, via scrolling, the way it was
       always meant to. Harmless on the few things this also touches that
       don't need it (the hidden radio inputs are position:absolute,
       already out of flex flow entirely; display:none tab panels don't
       participate in layout regardless of any other property). */
    :global(.mobile-content > *:last-child > *) {
      flex-shrink: 0;
    }

    /* fill-mode was `both` — WRONG, found live via a real-device debug
       log: minimize/maximize's own inline transform/opacity writes were
       silently having zero visual effect, even though the DOM (dataset,
       inline style attribute) all showed the correct minimized state.
       Root cause: per the CSS cascade, "CSS Animations" rank ABOVE plain
       author styles, inline styles included — `both`/`forwards` keeps
       this animation's ending keyframe (opacity:1, transform:none)
       permanently in force for as long as :has(.m-titlebar) keeps
       matching (i.e. forever, since the titlebar is never removed), which
       silently wins over any later `sheet.style.transform`/`opacity`
       write from MobileTitlebar.astro's setMobileMinimized/flip — the
       entrance animation was permanently locking out both. No fill-mode
       needed here anyway: with no animation-delay, there's no gap for
       `backwards` to cover, and the animation's own end keyframe already
       matches the element's normal resting style, so dropping fill
       entirely plays the identical one-time entrance and then gets out
       of the way, handing transform/opacity back to normal cascade
       (inline styles) the instant it's done. */
    /* All the floating-window chrome lives here now, scoped to pages that
       actually have a titlebar — see the base .mobile-content rule's
       comment above. Top margin at 70px (not just a safe-area allowance)
       specifically so it reads closer to the ~110px the bottom NEEDS for
       dock clearance — user feedback: "keep the window kind of centred
       currently it is leaning more toward the top." Genuinely equal
       top/bottom isn't practical (the bottom has a hard floor the dock
       imposes; the top doesn't), but closing most of that 90px gap down to
       ~40px reads as balanced rather than pinned to the top with the
       dock's own clearance dumped entirely below it. height's subtracted
       total (220px) matches that top+bottom margin sum plus a ~40px
       buffer. padding-top:0 overrides the base rule's own safe-area
       padding — margin's top value already covers that here, so both
       would double up the allowance otherwise. */
    .mobile-content:has(.m-titlebar) {
      height: calc(100dvh - 220px);
      margin: max(70px, env(safe-area-inset-top, 0px)) 16px max(110px, calc(env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px) + 100px));
      padding-top: 0;
      background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.62);
      backdrop-filter: blur(28px);
      -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(28px);
      border-radius: 20px;
      box-shadow: 0px 4px 20px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.12);
      transition: border-radius 0.35s ease, box-shadow 0.35s ease;
      animation: mobile-sheet-open 0.4s cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);
    }

    @keyframes mobile-sheet-open {
      from {
        opacity: 0;
        transform: translateY(28px) scale(0.97);
      }
      to {
        opacity: 1;
        transform: translateY(0) scale(1);
      }
    }

    /* Above phone width (this breakpoint now reaches up to portrait
       tablets, see the 1024px media query below), letting text/cards run
       edge-to-edge full-bleed the way they do on a 390px phone stretches
       body copy to an ~800px line length and spreads project cards/tap
       targets thin — capping the sheet's own width and centering it reads
       as a deliberate tablet layout instead of "the phone version, just
       wider." Phones stay exactly as they were: this only engages past
       600px, wider than any actual phone in portrait. Only overrides the
       left/right longhands, so the base rule's top/bottom margin (and its
       env(safe-area-inset) allowances) still apply here too. */
    @media (min-width: 600px) {
      .mobile-content {
        max-width: 600px;
        margin-left: auto;
        margin-right: auto;
      }
    }

    /* Real maximize (MobileTitlebar.astro's green button) — the sheet
       becomes the whole screen: no margin/peek on any side, no rounded
       corners, no tablet width cap, tall enough to cover the viewport
       even if its own content is shorter. Deliberately a large,
       unmissable visual jump from both the resting state (now a genuinely
       smaller floating window, see above) AND from minimized (opacity-0,
       shrunk to nothing) — the three states need to read as clearly
       different tiers, not subtle variations of each other. Higher
       specificity ([data-maximized="true"] adds an attribute selector)
       than the min-width:600px tablet-cap rule above, so this wins at any
       viewport width regardless of source order. */
    .mobile-content[data-maximized='true'] {
      margin: 0;
      max-width: none;
      height: 100dvh;
      border-radius: 0;
      box-shadow: none;
    }

    /* 1024px, not a phone-only ~600px cutoff: below this, the alternative
       is the scaled-down DESKTOP composition (.scale-frame, still 1440px
       wide, just shrunk via --frame-scale) — at a portrait tablet's own
       width (768-834px for iPad mini/Air/Pro 11", 1024px for iPad Pro
       12.9" portrait) that scale factor is ~0.53-0.72, small enough to
       shrink the 10px traffic-light dots and 13-16px body text down to
       genuinely hard-to-hit/hard-to-read sizes, while still being "close
       enough" to full size that it doesn't obviously read as "the mobile
       version" the way it does on a phone — the worst of both. Routing
       those widths into .mobile-shell instead means they get the same
       flex-column, real-touch-target layout a phone gets (with its own
       max-width cap above phone width — see .mobile-content/.mobile-dock)
       rather than a miniaturized desktop. Landscape tablets are left on
       the desktop path: at that aspect ratio the available width (1024px+)
       scales the 1440-wide frame close enough to 1:1 that it's genuinely
       the better-fitting experience, not a compromise. */
    @media (max-width: 1024px) {
      .scale-viewport {
        display: none;
      }

      .mobile-shell {
        display: block;
      }
    }

    .scale-viewport[data-scale-mode='fit-both'] {
      height: 100vh;
      align-items: center;
    }

    /* transform reads a custom property set on <html> (see the script below)
       rather than being set directly on this element. <html> is never
       replaced by a client-side navigation, so the property — and the
       correct scale it holds, since the viewport hasn't actually changed
       size — survives automatically onto a freshly-swapped-in .scale-frame
       from its very first rendered frame. Setting the transform directly on
       .scale-frame instead means each new instance starts unscaled until
       whichever script/event gets to it first, and no event turned out to
       reliably beat the View Transition's own snapshot of that unscaled
       state — which is what showed up as the window looking cut off from
       the top right after a dock-link navigation. */
    .scale-frame {
      width: 1440px;
      flex-shrink: 0;
      transform-origin: top center;
      transform: scale(var(--frame-scale, 1));
      /* Matches every individual Figma frame's own clipping (each "Desktop -
         NN" frame in the source clips its own content at its 1440x1024
         bounds — see the music widget's collapsed state, which is only a
         sliver because its own frame cuts it off at x:1440). Without this,
         .scale-frame's actual content can extend past its 1440-wide box into
         the empty margin .scale-viewport centers it within — found live: the
         music widget's right:-74px correctly computed, but rendered fully
         visible, floating with a gap past the window instead of clipped
         flush against the frame edge, because .scale-viewport's own
         overflow:hidden only clips at the real *browser window* edge (it's
         full viewport width, not 1440-frame width), which sits well beyond
         where .scale-frame's narrower, centered box actually ends. */
      overflow: hidden;
    }

    /* fit-both centers on both axes via flexbox, which aligns using the
       frame's *unscaled* box — the scale must originate from that same
       center point, or the visual result drifts off-center as scale changes. */
    .scale-viewport[data-scale-mode='fit-both'] .scale-frame {
      transform-origin: center center;
    }

    /* Case-study media lightbox: sits outside .scale-frame so the enlarged
       image/video renders at real viewport size, independent of the page's
       fit scale. Click any .cs-content img or video to open — only one of
       .lightbox-image/.lightbox-video is ever visible at a time (see
       openLightboxImage/openLightboxVideo below), the other stays
       display:none so an empty <video> or <img> never reserves layout
       space or shows a broken-media icon. */
    .lightbox {
      position: fixed;
      inset: 0;
      z-index: 10000;
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      background: rgba(20, 17, 13, 0.82);
      opacity: 0;
      visibility: hidden;
      pointer-events: none;
      transition: opacity 0.25s ease-out, visibility 0s linear 0.25s;
    }

    .lightbox.is-open {
      opacity: 1;
      visibility: visible;
      pointer-events: auto;
      transition: opacity 0.25s ease-out, visibility 0s linear 0s;
    }

    .lightbox-image,
    .lightbox-video {
      max-width: 88vw;
      max-height: 86vh;
      object-fit: contain;
      border-radius: 16px;
      box-shadow: 0px 24px 60px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);
      transform: scale(0.94);
      transition: transform 0.25s ease-out;
    }

    .lightbox.is-open .lightbox-image,
    .lightbox.is-open .lightbox-video {
      transform: scale(1);
    }

    .lightbox-close {
      position: absolute;
      top: 28px;
      right: 36px;
      width: 40px;
      height: 40px;
      border-radius: 50%;
      border: none;
      background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.12);
      color: #fff;
      font-size: 26px;
      line-height: 1;
      cursor: pointer;
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      transition: background-color 0.15s ease;
    }

    .lightbox-close:hover {
      background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.22);
    }

    /* Custom cursor removed — went through three different approaches
       (a JS-tracked blend-mode div, a native cursor:url() swap, a
       glass-blur DOM element) and landed back on "leave it, let's go back
       to the default cursor". Back to the plain OS default, no override. */
  </style>

  <style is:global>
    /* Page-transition timings, matching the Figma prototype's Smart Animate specs:
       0.3s ease-out is the default baseline for every named group here. The
       slower 1.0s tier (Home illustration-cluster links, and the Home entry/
       exit sequencing below) and the leaving-home/entering-home sequencing
       itself are NOT set with static classed selectors here — see
       #nav-sequence-style in <head> and buildNavSequenceCSS in the script
       below for why: astro:before-preparation is the only point early enough
       to compute them, but swapRootAttributes wipes any class set on <html>
       before the browser ever gets to decide which ::view-transition-*()
       animation CSS applies, so anything conditional has to live in that
       persisted stylesheet instead of a toggled class here.

       ::view-transition-*(active-window) (intro.astro's/work.astro's shared
       transition:name, see intro.astro's .intro-window comment),
       ::view-transition-*(home-toggle) (Nav.astro's .hero-nav — Home's
       View/Play switch, its only content), and ::view-transition-*(page-backdrop)
       (this file's #page-backdrop) are included in this DEFAULT rule too —
       without it, Astro gives every named transition:name group its own
       untouched default animation (180ms cubic-bezier, its stock "fade"
       preset) totally independent of whatever duration the surrounding
       root crossfade actually runs at, which read as a flicker every time a
       window-bearing page opened. Un-layered rules always beat Astro's own
       (its defaults live inside `@layer astro`), so this doesn't need any
       extra specificity to win. */
    ::view-transition-old(root),
    ::view-transition-new(root),
    ::view-transition-old(active-window),
    ::view-transition-new(active-window),
    ::view-transition-old(home-toggle),
    ::view-transition-new(home-toggle),
    ::view-transition-old(page-backdrop),
    ::view-transition-new(page-backdrop) {
      animation-duration: 0.3s;
      animation-timing-function: ease-out;
    }

    /* Home's window arrives ON TOP of its desk scene (still fully visible,
       not separately animated — see buildNavSequenceCSS below for why: it
       just gets cut once the window's done covering it, rather than
       animating away on its own), so it needs to actually paint above
       root's own layer rather than relying on default document-order
       stacking. Harmless for every other active-window transition (dock
       nav, case studies) where nothing sits underneath it to begin with. */
    ::view-transition-group(active-window) {
      z-index: 2;
    }

    /* Astro's default crossfade (astroFadeIn/astroFadeOut in
       astro/components/viewtransitions.css) sets mix-blend-mode:plus-lighter
       on both snapshots — the standard trick for crossfading thin content
       like text without a ghosted double-exposure look, since additive
       blending hides the moment both layers are simultaneously
       semi-transparent instead of letting them show through each other. The
       window is the opposite case: a large translucent white panel
       (rgba(255,255,255,0.58) background) over a photo backdrop, so that
       same additive blending visibly brightens/washes out the whole overlap
       region for the length of the crossfade — worse now that it runs a
       full 0.3s-1s instead of Astro's original unsynced 180ms, since
       there's simply more time for the wash-out to be seen. A plain normal
       blend (the two snapshots just alpha-composite over each other, no
       brightness boost) is the right call for this kind of large opaque-ish
       area. */
    ::view-transition-old(active-window),
    ::view-transition-new(active-window),
    ::view-transition-old(home-toggle),
    ::view-transition-new(home-toggle),
    ::view-transition-old(page-backdrop),
    ::view-transition-new(page-backdrop) {
      mix-blend-mode: normal;
    }

    /* Home <-> a destination page. Forward: only the View/Play toggle
       (Nav.astro's .hero-nav — a small bar above the desk scene, never
       overlapping where the window sits, so the window can't cover it the
       way it covers everything below) gets its own exit, sliding straight
       up and out of frame. The desk scene itself isn't separately animated
       at all — the window arrives on top of it (z-index above, see
       ::view-transition-group(active-window) above) and simply covers it as
       it scales in; the scene only actually gets cut once the window has
       fully finished, at which point it's already hidden underneath anyway.
       Reverse: the window shrinks/fades away first, revealing the scene
       that was there underneath the whole time, and the toggle slides back
       down. Static keyframes only — the sequencing itself (animation-name/
       -delay, which direction if any applies, actual durations) is written
       into #nav-sequence-style per navigation; see buildNavSequenceCSS
       below. */
    @keyframes toggle-slide-up {
      from {
        transform: translateY(0);
      }
      to {
        transform: translateY(-100%);
      }
    }

    @keyframes toggle-slide-down {
      from {
        transform: translateY(-100%);
      }
      to {
        transform: translateY(0);
      }
    }

    @keyframes window-scale-in {
      from {
        opacity: 0;
        scale: 0.96;
      }
      to {
        opacity: 1;
        scale: 1;
      }
    }

    @keyframes window-scale-out {
      from {
        opacity: 1;
        scale: 1;
      }
      to {
        opacity: 0;
        scale: 0.96;
      }
    }

    /* Both currently unused — see buildNavSequenceCSS's default branch
       below. Explored for ordinary dock-to-dock navigation (Work <-> Music
       <-> a case study, etc.) as a lighter stand-in for real multi-window
       (there is still only ever one .window in the DOM either way; every
       navigation fully replaces it, not a real stack): first as a two-sided
       motion reusing Window.astro's setMinimized() shape (new window
       grows+fades in from near the dock while old shrinks+fades toward it,
       concurrently), then as new-only once the two-sided version read as
       "crowded". Reverted entirely per direct feedback asking for the
       plain fade this had before any of this — left both keyframes defined
       rather than deleted in case a later tweak wants to reach for this
       shape again instead of reinventing it. */
    @keyframes window-cascade-in {
      from {
        opacity: 0;
        transform: scale(0.04) translateY(45%);
      }
      to {
        opacity: 1;
        transform: scale(1) translateY(0);
      }
    }

    @keyframes window-cascade-out {
      from {
        opacity: 1;
        transform: scale(1) translateY(0);
      }
      to {
        opacity: 0;
        transform: scale(0.04) translateY(45%);
      }
    }
  </style>

  <!-- Holds every navigation's actual duration/sequencing CSS, written into
       its textContent per-navigation from astro:before-preparation (see
       buildNavSequenceCSS in the script below) instead of toggled as
       classes on <html> — confirmed live that classList.toggle there
       doesn't survive to when it'd matter: swapRootAttributes (astro/dist/
       transitions/swap-functions.js) wipes every attribute on <html> during
       the DOM swap, which happens before the browser ever decides which
       ::view-transition-*() animation CSS applies (only resolved after the
       update callback — the same callback the wipe happens inside —
       resolves). Same root cause, same fix shape as --frame-scale's own
       #frame-scale-style (<head>): a transition:persist'd node's textContent
       survives every navigation untouched, so there's nothing to race.
       Placed here, AFTER the static is:global rules above rather than in
       <head> — on a tie, an unlayered rule's specificity is identical
       regardless of which stylesheet it's in, so the one later in DOM order
       wins; this one needs to win over the static default duration rule
       above it, and being later in source order is what makes that happen,
       not anything about being un-layered (both blocks already are). -->
  <style id="nav-sequence-style" is:inline transition:name="nav-sequence-style" transition:persist></style>

  <script>
    // Astro's own ClientRouter (node_modules/astro/dist/transitions/router.js,
    // astro 5.18.2) calls document.startViewTransition() and attaches
    // .finally() handlers to the returned ViewTransition's updateCallbackDone
    // and finished promises — both of which the actual page swap and
    // animation depend on — but never touches its .ready promise anywhere.
    // .ready legitimately rejects in several browser-internal cases (e.g.
    // the document not being visible at the moment the transition starts),
    // without affecting the swap/animation at all, so this is a harmless but
    // real gap in Astro's shipped code, not patchable here without editing
    // node_modules (which a reinstall would wipe). Silence specifically this
    // known rejection so it doesn't spam the console; anything else still
    // surfaces normally.
    //
    // (A follow-up theory that this rejection also left the
    // ::view-transition-old/new(root) pseudo-elements stuck on screen,
    // causing a washed-out double-exposure look, did not hold up:
    // getComputedStyle(el, '::view-transition-old(root)') returns the same
    // display:block/opacity:1 even on a completely fresh page load with no
    // navigation ever having happened — it's a hypothetical/stylesheet-
    // derived value regardless of whether the pseudo-element currently
    // exists, not evidence of anything stuck. Root cause of the washed-out/
    // cut-off report is still open.)
    window.addEventListener('unhandledrejection', (e) => {
      if (e.reason instanceof DOMException && e.reason.name === 'InvalidStateError' && /transition/i.test(e.reason.message)) {
        e.preventDefault();
      }
    });

    function fitScale() {
      if (window.innerWidth <= 1024) return; // mobile-shell is active instead; nothing to scale
      const viewport = document.querySelector('.scale-viewport');
      const frame = document.querySelector('.scale-frame');
      if (!viewport || !frame) return;

      const mode = viewport.getAttribute('data-scale-mode');
      const naturalWidth = 1440;

      // Measuring natural height needs the *unscaled* box, but reading that
      // off the frame itself would require zeroing out --frame-scale first
      // (visible, and only reads back correctly after a reflow) — reading
      // the un-transformed child in fit-both mode instead (natural height is
      // always exactly 1024 there anyway) sidesteps needing to touch the
      // custom property just to measure.
      const naturalHeight = mode === 'fit-both' ? 1024 : frame.offsetHeight;

      const vw = window.innerWidth;
      const vh = window.innerHeight;

      const scale =
        mode === 'fit-both'
          ? Math.min(vw / naturalWidth, vh / naturalHeight, 1)
          : Math.min(vw / naturalWidth, 1);

      const styleEl = document.getElementById('frame-scale-style');
      if (styleEl) styleEl.textContent = `:root{--frame-scale:${scale}}`;
      if (mode !== 'fit-both') {
        viewport.style.height = `${naturalHeight * scale}px`;
      }
    }

    let resizeTimer;
    function scheduleFit() {
      clearTimeout(resizeTimer);
      resizeTimer = setTimeout(fitScale, 50);
    }

    window.addEventListener('resize', scheduleFit);
    // Fit on every lifecycle event this could plausibly need it — astro:after-swap
    // has the new page's DOM in place but hasn't painted yet, astro:page-load is
    // the final "settled" point. #frame-scale-style (see <head>) is what actually
    // keeps the value alive across navigations now; these two calls just keep it
    // numerically correct (e.g. after a resize that happened mid-navigation).
    document.addEventListener('astro:after-swap', fitScale);
    document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', fitScale);

    // Toggling between alternate full-screen panels (e.g. Home's View/Play switch)
    // changes the frame's natural height without a page navigation — refit.
    document.addEventListener('change', (e) => {
      if (e.target instanceof HTMLInputElement && (e.target.type === 'radio' || e.target.type === 'checkbox')) {
        fitScale();
      }
    });

    // Links marked [data-transition-slow] (Home's illustration cluster, plus
    // the two "return to Home" triggers — Dock's Home icon and Window's red
    // close button) use the slower 1.0s page-transition timing instead of
    // the 0.3s default.
    //
    // Home <-> a destination page also gets its own dedicated sequencing —
    // currently the simplest version of it, deliberately, as an A/B
    // reference point: everything (desk scene, toggle, window, backdrop)
    // does one plain synchronized fade together, and the only sequencing
    // kept is the dock still arriving afterward, not simultaneously with
    // the rest. A more elaborate staged version (toggle slides out first,
    // window scales in on top of the still-visible desk scene and covers
    // it before it's cut, backdrop timed to arrive just ahead of the
    // window) was built and discarded here specifically to compare against
    // this simpler baseline — reach for that shape again (window-scale-in/
    // -out and toggle-slide-up/-down keyframes are still defined below,
    // unused, for exactly this reason) if the plain version reads as too
    // flat rather than too busy. intro.astro is deliberately excluded from
    // all of this either way: its own [data-beat] sequence already
    // controls that exact handoff frame by frame.
    //
    // All of this is written into #nav-sequence-style's textContent (see
    // <head>) instead of toggled as classes on <html> — confirmed live that
    // classList.toggle here doesn't survive to when it'd matter:
    // astro:before-preparation is the only point early enough to compute
    // "is this link slow / is this leaving or entering Home", since by
    // astro:before-swap the click has already resolved to a real navigation
    // — but Astro's own router wipes every attribute on <html> (swapRootAttributes
    // in astro/dist/transitions/swap-functions.js) during the DOM swap,
    // which happens before the browser ever decides which ::view-transition-*()
    // animation CSS applies (that's resolved only after the update callback
    // — the same callback the wipe happens inside — resolves). Logged
    // document.documentElement.className from inside an actual
    // astro:after-swap handler during a real navigation and it was already ""
    // — same root cause, same fix shape as --frame-scale's own #frame-scale-style.
    //
    // isSlow (1s) only applies as a fallback for triggers that aren't part
    // of this Home entry/exit sequencing at all (currently none besides
    // intro.astro's own synthetic navigate() call into /work, which sets
    // data-transition-slow but is excluded from leaving/entering by the
    // toPath/fromPath !== '/intro' checks below — its [data-beat] sequence
    // needs active-window's root-level crossfade slowed to 1s too so its
    // own beats don't finish before the surrounding page catches up).
    const FADE_S = 0.35;

    function buildNavSequenceCSS(isSlow, leaving, entering, introToWork) {
      let css = '';
      // Mobile's own intro sequence ends by scaling a fake "Work window"
      // up to the EXACT position/size the real /work page's own window
      // resolves to (intro.astro's playMobileIntro), specifically so the
      // handoff reads as "content just appeared in the same window" —
      // direct feedback after the first version: "why is there a big
      // switch kind of transition... should be smooth like nothing
      // happened, just different content faded in." The pixel-match
      // alone wasn't enough: .mobile-content is never persisted (a fresh
      // instance mounts every navigation, unlike desktop's real <Window>,
      // which literally survives via transition:persist), so the
      // incoming /work page still replayed its own mobile-sheet-open
      // entrance (fade+scale+translateY from below) ON TOP of the root
      // crossfade — two motions stacked instead of one, which is what
      // actually read as "a switch." Suppressing that one animation for
      // this specific navigation only (mobile-content still fades in via
      // the root crossfade itself, just without ALSO bouncing) is the
      // fix; can't do this with a static CSS rule since it depends on
      // knowing the previous page was /intro, hence adding it here
      // alongside the leaving/entering Home cases, which solve the exact
      // same "know the nav direction before the DOM swap" problem.
      if (introToWork) {
        // !important is load-bearing here, not stylistic: the scoped
        // component rule this overrides is
        // `.mobile-content[data-astro-cid-XXXX]:has(.m-titlebar)` (class +
        // attribute + pseudo-class), which outranks a plain
        // `.mobile-content:has(.m-titlebar)` on specificity alone regardless
        // of injection order — confirmed live (rule was present in
        // #nav-sequence-style but had zero effect on computed animationName
        // until this was added).
        css += `.mobile-content:has(.m-titlebar){animation:none!important}`;
      }
      if (leaving) {
        css += `::view-transition-old(root),::view-transition-new(root),::view-transition-old(home-toggle),::view-transition-new(home-toggle),::view-transition-old(active-window),::view-transition-new(active-window),::view-transition-old(page-backdrop),::view-transition-new(page-backdrop){animation-duration:${FADE_S}s}`;
      } else if (entering) {
        css += `::view-transition-old(root),::view-transition-new(root),::view-transition-old(home-toggle),::view-transition-new(home-toggle),::view-transition-old(active-window),::view-transition-new(active-window){animation-duration:${FADE_S}s}`;
      } else if (isSlow) {
        css += `::view-transition-old(root),::view-transition-new(root),::view-transition-old(active-window),::view-transition-new(active-window){animation-duration:1s}`;
      }
      // Ordinary AppLayout<->AppLayout navigation (dock link to dock link,
      // or into/out of a case study) falls through to an empty string here
      // on purpose — a window-cascade-in/-out pair (tried both as a
      // two-sided motion and as new-only) was built and explicitly reverted
      // per direct feedback ("pehle hi thik tha, sirf minimize mei animation
      // rakho baaki pehle jaisa kardo fade wala jo tha" — it was fine
      // before, keep the animation only on minimize, put everything else
      // back to the plain fade). Reverting to nothing here means this kind
      // of navigation is governed entirely by the unconditional base rule
      // above (0.3s ease-out on ::view-transition-old/new(active-window)),
      // the same plain crossfade this had before any of that exploration.
      // The keyframes themselves (window-cascade-in/-out, static is:global
      // block above) are left defined, unused, in case this gets revisited.
      return css;
    }

    document.addEventListener('astro:before-preparation', (e) => {
      const trigger = e.sourceElement;
      const isSlow = trigger instanceof Element && !!trigger.closest('[data-transition-slow]');
      const toPath = e.to?.pathname;
      const fromPath = e.from?.pathname;
      const leaving = fromPath === '/' && toPath !== '/' && toPath !== '/intro';
      const entering = toPath === '/' && fromPath !== '/' && fromPath !== '/intro';
      const introToWork = fromPath === '/intro' && toPath === '/work';
      const seqStyleEl = document.getElementById('nav-sequence-style');
      if (seqStyleEl) seqStyleEl.textContent = buildNavSequenceCSS(isSlow, leaving, entering, introToWork);

      // AppLayout's dock-entrance script (initDockEntrance) reads this to
      // delay its own start until AFTER the window has finished scaling
      // in, instead of starting immediately on astro:page-load — which
      // fires (and so previously kicked off the dock's fade+slide-up)
      // right as the DOM swap completes, well before the View Transition's
      // own crossfade even begins playing, landing the dock on screen
      // before the window it's supposed to follow. A plain window global
      // survives the navigation fine (nothing about swapRootAttributes'
      // wipe touches window/document identity, only <html>'s attributes),
      // so no persisted-stylesheet trick is needed for this one.
      window.__dockEntranceDelayMs = leaving ? FADE_S * 1000 : 0;
    });

    // Case-study media lightbox. Bound once on the persistent `document` —
    // this script re-runs on every Astro navigation (the #lightbox element
    // itself is replaced each time), so re-binding would stack duplicate
    // listeners; the flag below guards against that and every lookup below
    // re-queries the current #lightbox instead of closing over a stale node.
    if (!window.__lightboxInit) {
      window.__lightboxInit = true;

      const openLightboxImage = (src, alt) => {
        const overlay = document.getElementById('lightbox');
        const imgEl = overlay?.querySelector('.lightbox-image');
        const videoEl = overlay?.querySelector('.lightbox-video');
        if (!overlay || !(imgEl instanceof HTMLImageElement) || !(videoEl instanceof HTMLVideoElement)) return;
        videoEl.pause();
        videoEl.removeAttribute('src');
        videoEl.style.display = 'none';
        imgEl.style.display = '';
        imgEl.src = src;
        imgEl.alt = alt;
        overlay.classList.add('is-open');
        overlay.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'false');
      };

      // Enlarged the same way an image does, but playing — direct
      // instruction: case-study videos should open into a larger frame like
      // images do, not sit inert. Muted/loop, matching every other autoplay
      // clip on the site (the reel filmstrip's own hero video included) —
      // no site video anywhere plays with sound, so this stays consistent
      // rather than introducing the one exception.
      //
      // radius carries over the SAME border-radius the small inline slot
      // used (read off its .phone-shot-empty container at the call site
      // below), rather than the flat 16px .lightbox-image uses — direct
      // instruction: the large overlay should crop corners "the same way"
      // as the small form, not with a generic photo-lightbox rounding that
      // doesn't match the phone-shaped mockup slots (ph-hero/ph-tall/
      // ph-full are 60px, ph-control is 30px).
      const openLightboxVideo = (src, radius) => {
        const overlay = document.getElementById('lightbox');
        const imgEl = overlay?.querySelector('.lightbox-image');
        const videoEl = overlay?.querySelector('.lightbox-video');
        if (!overlay || !(imgEl instanceof HTMLImageElement) || !(videoEl instanceof HTMLVideoElement)) return;
        imgEl.style.display = 'none';
        imgEl.removeAttribute('src');
        videoEl.style.display = '';
        videoEl.style.borderRadius = radius || '';
        videoEl.src = src;
        videoEl.play().catch(() => {});
        overlay.classList.add('is-open');
        overlay.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'false');
      };

      const closeLightbox = () => {
        const overlay = document.getElementById('lightbox');
        overlay?.querySelector('.lightbox-video')?.pause();
        overlay?.classList.remove('is-open');
        overlay?.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      };

      document.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
        const target = e.target;
        if (!(target instanceof Element)) return;

        if (target.closest('.lightbox')) {
          if (target.closest('.lightbox-close') || target.id === 'lightbox') closeLightbox();
          return;
        }

        const img = target.closest('.cs-content img');
        if (img instanceof HTMLImageElement) {
          openLightboxImage(img.currentSrc || img.src, img.alt);
          return;
        }

        const video = target.closest('.cs-content video');
        if (video instanceof HTMLVideoElement) {
          const slot = video.closest('.phone-shot-empty');
          const radius = slot ? getComputedStyle(slot).borderRadius : '';
          openLightboxVideo(video.currentSrc || video.src, radius);
        }
      });

      document.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
        if (e.key === 'Escape') closeLightbox();
      });

      document.addEventListener('astro:before-preparation', closeLightbox);
    }

    // Custom cursor removed (see <style> above) — back to the plain
    // default, no JS needed.

    // Global "now playing" widget (see the markup + <style> above this
    // script, and node 2410:10745 in Figma). #music-sc-iframe/#music-widget
    // both transition:persist, so this only needs to bind its listeners
    // ONCE ever (guarded exactly like #lightbox above) — re-running per
    // navigation would stack duplicate SC.Widget event bindings on the same
    // surviving iframe.
    if (!window.__musicWidgetInit) {
      window.__musicWidgetInit = true;

      // Playback engine: SoundCloud's Widget JS API wrapping the iframe
      // above, not an <audio> element — direct instruction after the
      // earlier placeholder-file version: "you can use the soundcloud api
      // ... player can control the calls instead. some kind of wrapper."
      // scWidget is null until SC.Widget(scIframe) below resolves, and
      // "not ready" until its own READY event fires (SoundCloud has to load
      // the track first) — every function below checks scReady rather than
      // assuming scWidget is immediately usable the instant it's created.
      // Created once here and appended to <html> itself (a sibling of
      // <body>, never a descendant of it) rather than rendered as template
      // markup inside <body> — see the long comment where the <iframe> used
      // to sit, above the SoundCloud <script> tag near the top of this
      // file, for why: Astro's swapBodyElement detaches everything inside
      // the old <body> for a moment on every navigation, and an iframe
      // resets its whole browsing context on disconnection regardless of
      // transition:persist correctly preserving the node's identity
      // afterward. Living outside <body> means the swap never touches it at
      // all. This whole block only ever runs once (the __musicWidgetInit
      // guard around this entire init block), so there's no risk of
      // creating a second iframe on a later navigation.

      // Real track list (was a single hardcoded track) — TRACKS is the one
      // source of truth for both playback (scWidget.load(), see loadTrack
      // below) and the queue panel's markup (see renderQueue below), so
      // adding a third song later only ever means pushing one more entry
      // here.
      const TRACKS = [
        {
          title: 'Good Days',
          artist: 'SZA',
          cover: '/images/music/good-days-cover.png',
          url: 'https://soundcloud.com/qwarqrtt43/good-days-sza-best-173940637',
        },
        {
          title: 'Starry Eyes',
          artist: 'Cigarettes After Sex',
          cover: '/images/music/starry-eyes-cover.png',
          url: 'https://soundcloud.com/cigarettesaftersex/04-starry-eyes-mp3',
        },
      ];
      let currentTrackIndex = 0;

      // Only used for the iframe's own bootstrap src (the initial track) —
      // subsequent track switches go through scWidget.load(), which takes
      // the raw track url plus a separate options object instead of this
      // wrapped/query-string form.
      function scPlayerSrc(trackUrl) {
        return (
          'https://w.soundcloud.com/player/?url=' +
          encodeURIComponent(trackUrl) +
          '&auto_play=false&hide_related=true&show_comments=false&show_user=false&show_reposts=false&show_teaser=false&visual=false'
        );
      }

      const scIframe = document.createElement('iframe');
      scIframe.id = 'music-sc-iframe';
      scIframe.title = TRACKS[0].title + ' by ' + TRACKS[0].artist + ' - SoundCloud player';
      scIframe.src = scPlayerSrc(TRACKS[0].url);
      scIframe.style.cssText = 'position:fixed; left:-9999px; top:-9999px; width:1px; height:1px; border:none;';
      scIframe.setAttribute('allow', 'autoplay');
      document.documentElement.appendChild(scIframe);

      let scWidget = null;
      let scReady = false;
      let scDurationMs = 0;
      let scPositionMs = 0;
      let scPlayingState = false;
      let scVolume = 1;
      let scMuted = false;

      const widget = document.getElementById('music-widget');
      const widgetIcon = document.getElementById('music-widget-icon-btn');
      const widgetArtImg = document.getElementById('music-widget-art-img');
      const widgetArtBtn = document.getElementById('music-widget-art-btn');

      // Everything below is inside #music-overlay, which is NOT
      // transition:persist (that combination with this card's own
      // backdrop-filter blur corrupts the compositor during page
      // transitions — confirmed live, reverted once already). Astro
      // replaces this whole subtree with a fresh, listener-less copy on
      // every client-side navigation, so these are `let`, not `const` —
      // bindOverlayInteractions() (below) re-queries every one of them and
      // re-binds their direct listeners on every astro:page-load, not just
      // once at init. Root-caused live: "music player pe bhi click nahi
      // hota album wala until reload" — same shape as the video-autoplay
      // bug fixed earlier this session, just for DOM/listener staleness
      // instead of a failed media load.
      let overlay,
        overlayArtImg,
        overlayTitleEl,
        overlayArtistEl,
        overlayPlayBtn,
        overlayPrevBtn,
        overlayNextBtn,
        queuePanel,
        timelineTrack,
        timelineFill,
        timelineThumb,
        timeCurrentEl,
        timeDurationEl,
        muteBtn,
        volumeSlider,
        volumeControl,
        queueBtn;
      // Shared between the window-level pointermove/pointerup below (bound
      // ONCE — window itself is never replaced, so they don't need
      // rebinding) and timelineTrack's own pointerdown (rebound on every
      // navigation, see bindOverlayInteractions below) — both need to agree
      // on the same flag.
      let seekingTimeline = false;

      // Reflects the current track into every piece of UI that shows it —
      // the small widget's art/aria-label, the big overlay's art/title/
      // artist, and the queue panel's "Now playing" tag (via renderQueue).
      // Called once at init (for the starting track) and again by loadTrack
      // whenever prev/next/a queue item switches tracks.
      function updateNowPlayingUI() {
        const track = TRACKS[currentTrackIndex];
        if (!track) return;
        if (widgetArtImg instanceof HTMLImageElement) widgetArtImg.src = track.cover;
        widgetArtBtn?.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Now playing: ' + track.title + ' by ' + track.artist + ', open player');
        if (overlayArtImg instanceof HTMLImageElement) overlayArtImg.src = track.cover;
        if (overlayTitleEl) overlayTitleEl.textContent = track.title;
        if (overlayArtistEl) overlayArtistEl.textContent = track.artist;
        scIframe.title = track.title + ' by ' + track.artist + ' - SoundCloud player';
        renderQueue();
      }

      // Rebuilds the queue panel's items from TRACKS — the heading
      // (.music-overlay-queue-heading) is the only static child, so this
      // only ever removes/re-adds .music-overlay-queue-item siblings.
      // Clicking a non-current item switches to it (see the click
      // delegation on queuePanel further down); the current track keeps
      // playing/paused state across the switch via loadTrack's autoplay arg.
      function renderQueue() {
        if (!(queuePanel instanceof HTMLElement)) return;
        queuePanel.querySelectorAll('.music-overlay-queue-item').forEach((el) => el.remove());
        TRACKS.forEach((track, i) => {
          const item = document.createElement('div');
          item.className = 'music-overlay-queue-item';
          item.dataset.trackIndex = String(i);
          item.innerHTML =
            '<span class="music-overlay-queue-item-art"><img src="' + track.cover + '" alt="" /></span>' +
            '<span class="music-overlay-queue-item-meta">' +
            '<span class="music-overlay-queue-item-title">' + track.title + '</span>' +
            '<span class="music-overlay-queue-item-artist">' + track.artist + '</span>' +
            '</span>' +
            (i === currentTrackIndex ? '<span class="music-overlay-queue-item-tag">Now playing</span>' : '');
          queuePanel.appendChild(item);
        });
      }

      // Source of truth for "is playing" lives on the widget's own dataset
      // (survives navigation via transition:persist, same reasoning as
      // everHovered below), kept in sync by SC.Widget's own PLAY/PAUSE
      // events (see initSoundCloudWidget below) rather than read from
      // scWidget directly — scWidget's methods are fire-and-forget/async
      // (postMessage under the hood, no synchronous .paused-style property
      // to read), so the events are the only reliable signal.
      function setPlaying(playing) {
        if (widget instanceof HTMLElement) widget.dataset.playing = playing ? 'true' : 'false';
        overlayPlayBtn?.setAttribute('data-playing', playing ? 'true' : 'false');
        refreshIcon();
      }

      // Hover shows a one-time "chevron" affordance the very first time
      // ever (nothing has played yet, so there's no play/pause status to
      // show) — every hover after that reflects the current playing state
      // instead. Stored on the DOM node's dataset rather than a plain JS
      // variable because transition:persist keeps the NODE but this
      // script's own top-level scope still re-runs per navigation (see the
      // __musicWidgetInit guard above) — a closure variable would silently
      // reset to its initial value on the very next page.
      function refreshIcon() {
        if (!(widgetIcon instanceof HTMLElement) || !(widget instanceof HTMLElement)) return;
        if (widget.dataset.everHovered !== 'true') {
          widgetIcon.dataset.icon = 'chevron';
        } else {
          widgetIcon.dataset.icon = widget.dataset.playing === 'true' ? 'pause' : 'play';
        }
      }

      function setExpanded(expanded) {
        if (!(widget instanceof HTMLElement)) return;
        widget.classList.toggle('is-expanded', expanded);
        if (expanded && widget.dataset.everHovered !== 'true') {
          widget.dataset.everHovered = 'true';
        }
        refreshIcon();
      }

      function openOverlay() {
        overlay?.classList.add('is-open');
        overlay?.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'false');
      }

      function closeOverlay() {
        overlay?.classList.remove('is-open');
        overlay?.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
        // Reset so the queue panel doesn't reopen already-expanded next
        // time — queueBtn/queuePanel are declared further down in this same
        // init block; safe here since this function only ever RUNS later,
        // via an event listener, well after those declarations execute.
        document.getElementById('music-overlay-queue-panel')?.classList.remove('is-open');
        document.getElementById('music-overlay-queue')?.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'false');
        // Back to hidden-in-the-edge once the player closes, rather than
        // leaving the orb sitting expanded with no reason to be — matches
        // "hide it in edge like we did for desktop" as the resting state,
        // not just the initial one. No-op on desktop (already collapsed by
        // mouseleave well before a click could have opened the overlay).
        setExpanded(false);
      }

      // Route-gated: intro.astro recreates this same desk scene by hand for
      // its own beat sequence (see intro.astro) — the real widget floating
      // on top of it would double up on that illusion, so it's hidden for
      // exactly that one route. Re-checked on every navigation, since the
      // widget node persists across all of them.
      function refreshRouteVisibility() {
        widget?.classList.toggle('is-hidden-route', location.pathname === '/intro');
      }

      widget?.addEventListener('mouseenter', () => setExpanded(true));
      widget?.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => setExpanded(false));
      widget?.addEventListener('focusin', () => setExpanded(true));
      widget?.addEventListener('focusout', () => setExpanded(false));

      // Touch's equivalent of mouseleave — there's no hover to lose, so
      // nothing above ever collapses a tap-expanded orb back down. Tapping
      // anywhere outside it does instead, so a tap that expanded it but
      // wasn't followed by the second tap-to-open doesn't just leave it
      // stuck open with no way back except opening the player anyway.
      document.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
        if (!widget?.classList.contains('is-expanded')) return;
        if (e.target instanceof Element && widget.contains(e.target)) return;
        setExpanded(false);
      });

      // Vertical-only drag along the right edge (right:0 never changes,
      // only top) — direct instruction: "draggable along the right edge of
      // screen". Position persisted in localStorage (a per-browser
      // convenience, not real app state, so localStorage is the right tool
      // rather than anything server-synced) so it survives full reloads,
      // not just SPA navigation. A real drag vs. a plain click are told
      // apart by total pointer movement: past DRAG_CLICK_THRESHOLD, the
      // upcoming native 'click' event (which still fires after pointerup on
      // the same target) is suppressed for exactly that one gesture via
      // justDragged, so dragging doesn't also pop the overlay open.
      //
      // 6px (the first version) turned out too tight — reported back as
      // "not clickable, can't open the player overlay" after dragging
      // shipped. Root cause, confirmed live: pointermove was applied to
      // `top` on the very first pixel of movement, so ordinary click jitter
      // (a mouse or trackpad essentially never releases at the *exact*
      // pixel it pressed down on) already exceeded 6px and got misclassified
      // as a drag on plain clicks. Fixed two ways together: a bigger
      // threshold (12px), AND a real deadzone — pointermove no longer moves
      // the widget at all until past that threshold (see below), so a
      // normal click now has zero visual side effect instead of a tiny
      // unwanted nudge.
      const DRAG_CLICK_THRESHOLD = 12;
      const DRAG_STORAGE_KEY = 'music-widget-top';

      function clampTop(top) {
        if (!(widget instanceof HTMLElement)) return top;
        const margin = 20;
        const max = window.innerHeight - widget.offsetHeight - margin;
        return Math.min(Math.max(top, margin), Math.max(margin, max));
      }

      if (widget instanceof HTMLElement) {
        let dragging = false;
        let startY = 0;
        let startTop = 0;
        let moved = 0;

        try {
          const saved = localStorage.getItem(DRAG_STORAGE_KEY);
          if (saved !== null) {
            const top = parseFloat(saved);
            if (Number.isFinite(top)) widget.style.top = clampTop(top) + 'px';
          }
        } catch (e) {
          // Private-browsing/storage-blocked — fall back to the CSS default
          // top (see .music-widget), nothing else to do.
        }

        // pointermove/pointerup are bound on `window`, not `widget`, and
        // deliberately WITHOUT setPointerCapture — found live: capturing
        // the pointer on the outer widget redirects the click that follows
        // pointerup to target the capturing element instead of letting it
        // reach the inner #music-widget-trigger button through the normal
        // hit-test/bubble path, which is exactly what broke "click to open
        // the overlay" the moment dragging was added. Listening on `window`
        // instead achieves the same "track movement anywhere, even outside
        // the small widget" goal without touching event routing at all.
        widget.addEventListener('pointerdown', (e) => {
          if (typeof e.button === 'number' && e.button !== 0) return;
          // Direct instruction: the album cover should ONLY click (open the
          // overlay), never drag; same for the icon (only toggles
          // play/pause). Dragging happens by grabbing the rest of the
          // widget — the pill background outside both buttons — instead.
          // Since pointerdown bubbles up to this listener from EITHER
          // button, checking the target here (not just relying on
          // DRAG_CLICK_THRESHOLD after the fact) stops drag-tracking from
          // ever starting on a button press at all, rather than starting it
          // and then trying to suppress the click afterward.
          if (e.target instanceof Element && e.target.closest('#music-widget-art-btn, #music-widget-icon-btn')) {
            return;
          }
          dragging = true;
          moved = 0;
          startY = e.clientY;
          startTop = widget.getBoundingClientRect().top;
          widget.classList.add('is-dragging');
          // Belt-and-suspenders alongside draggable="false"/-webkit-user-drag
          // above: stops the browser's own native image-drag/text-selection
          // gestures from starting on mouse pointers at all, so they can't
          // compete with this custom drag for the same pointerdown. Touch
          // pointers are excluded — preventDefault on a touch pointerdown
          // also blocks the synthetic click touch devices rely on, which
          // would break tapping this on mobile (moot on desktop-only for
          // now, but no reason to paint into that corner).
          if (e.pointerType !== 'touch') e.preventDefault();
        });

        window.addEventListener('pointermove', (e) => {
          if (!dragging) return;
          const dy = e.clientY - startY;
          moved = Math.max(moved, Math.abs(dy));
          // Deadzone: don't actually move the widget until movement is
          // confirmed to be a real drag, not click jitter (see
          // DRAG_CLICK_THRESHOLD's own comment above).
          if (moved > DRAG_CLICK_THRESHOLD) {
            widget.style.top = clampTop(startTop + dy) + 'px';
          }
        });

        const endDrag = () => {
          if (!dragging) return;
          dragging = false;
          widget.classList.remove('is-dragging');
          if (moved > DRAG_CLICK_THRESHOLD) {
            widget.dataset.justDragged = 'true';
            // Self-clearing: the two button click handlers below also
            // delete this flag when they consume it, but that only happens
            // if the native 'click' that follows THIS pointerup actually
            // lands on one of those two buttons. If a real drag ends with
            // the pointer somewhere else on the widget (i.e. the drag
            // didn't finish exactly back over a button — the ordinary case,
            // not an edge case), that click never reaches either handler,
            // so justDragged was staying 'true' forever — permanently
            // jamming EVERY future click on both buttons after just one
            // drag, not just suppressing the one click it was meant to.
            // Matches the report: drag worked, then play/pause silently
            // stopped responding to any click at all afterward.
            setTimeout(() => {
              delete widget.dataset.justDragged;
            }, 300);
            try {
              localStorage.setItem(DRAG_STORAGE_KEY, parseFloat(widget.style.top) + '');
            } catch (e) {
              // Same private-browsing/storage-blocked case as above —
              // dragging this session still works, it just won't persist.
            }
          }
        };
        window.addEventListener('pointerup', endDrag);
        window.addEventListener('pointercancel', endDrag);

        window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
          if (widget.style.top) widget.style.top = clampTop(parseFloat(widget.style.top)) + 'px';
        });
      }

      // Consumed by both buttons below — a drag ending on either one also
      // fires a native 'click' afterward (see endDrag/DRAG_CLICK_THRESHOLD
      // above), which this swallows for exactly that one gesture so
      // dragging doesn't also trigger whichever button it ends on.
      function consumeJustDragged() {
        if (widget?.dataset.justDragged === 'true') {
          delete widget.dataset.justDragged;
          return true;
        }
        return false;
      }

      function togglePlay() {
        if (!scWidget || !scReady) return;
        // scPlayingState itself is set optimistically here, not just the UI
        // — found live: leaving it to be set ONLY by SC's own PLAY/PAUSE
        // events (a postMessage round-trip to the iframe, not instant) meant
        // a second click before that event arrived still saw the OLD
        // scPlayingState, so togglePlay took the same branch again — two
        // "play" calls in a row instead of play-then-pause. The real events
        // still correct this afterward if actual playback ever diverges
        // (autoplay blocked, paused some other way), same self-correcting
        // relationship the old <audio>-based version had.
        if (scPlayingState) {
          scWidget.pause();
          scPlayingState = false;
        } else {
          scWidget.play();
          scPlayingState = true;
        }
        setPlaying(scPlayingState);
      }

      // Two separate buttons now, two separate actions — direct correction
      // from the earlier "all one control" version: the icon should toggle
      // playback directly, only the album-art button opens the overlay.
      //
      // The is-expanded gate makes this the same click handler serve both
      // desktop and mobile's different reveal gestures without branching on
      // pointer type: on desktop, by the time a click on this button is
      // even possible the cursor is already over the widget, so :hover (see
      // mouseenter above) already set is-expanded — the gate is always true
      // there, first click opens the overlay exactly like before. On mobile
      // there's no hover, so is-expanded starts false — direct instruction:
      // "on hidden state tap, it comes out, and on one more tap, the player
      // opens" — first tap only expands (and returns, deliberately not
      // opening yet), second tap (now already expanded) falls through to
      // openOverlay().
      document.getElementById('music-widget-art-btn')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
        if (consumeJustDragged()) return;
        if (!widget?.classList.contains('is-expanded')) {
          setExpanded(true);
          return;
        }
        openOverlay();
      });

      document.getElementById('music-widget-icon-btn')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
        if (consumeJustDragged()) return;
        togglePlay();
      });

      // Real track-list navigation — swaps the widget's loaded track via
      // SC.Widget's own load(url, options), rather than recreating the
      // iframe (which would re-trigger the whole "detach resets browsing
      // context" problem the iframe's long init comment above describes).
      // autoplay carries the CURRENT playing state across the switch so
      // prev/next keeps playing if a track was already playing, or stays
      // paused if it wasn't — SoundCloud's own READY event only fires once
      // ever (on the very first track), so every load after that relies on
      // this call's own `callback` option to know when the new track is
      // actually ready, not another READY bind.
      function loadTrack(index, { autoplay = false } = {}) {
        const track = TRACKS[index];
        if (!track || !scWidget) return;
        currentTrackIndex = index;
        scDurationMs = 0;
        scPositionMs = 0;
        updateTimelineUI();
        updateNowPlayingUI();
        scWidget.load(track.url, {
          auto_play: autoplay,
          hide_related: true,
          show_comments: false,
          show_user: false,
          show_reposts: false,
          show_teaser: false,
          visual: false,
          callback: () => {
            scReady = true;
            scWidget.getDuration((ms) => {
              scDurationMs = ms;
              updateTimelineUI();
            });
            scWidget.setVolume(scMuted ? 0 : scVolume * 100);
            if (autoplay) {
              scPlayingState = true;
              setPlaying(true);
            }
          },
        });
      }

      // Timeline/seek bar. Big-overlay-only, per direct instruction (the
      // small widget stays icon-only). Draggable the same deadzone-free way
      // as the click-through-a-drag problem on the widget itself was fixed
      // — window-level pointermove/pointerup rather than setPointerCapture,
      // since capture was what broke the widget's own click routing.

      function formatTime(seconds) {
        if (!Number.isFinite(seconds) || seconds < 0) return '0:00';
        const m = Math.floor(seconds / 60);
        const s = Math.floor(seconds % 60);
        return `${m}:${s < 10 ? '0' : ''}${s}`;
      }

      function updateTimelineUI() {
        // scDurationMs is 0 until SC.Widget's READY event resolves
        // getDuration() (see initSoundCloudWidget below) — guarded
        // explicitly rather than showing "NaN:NaN" or a fill computed from
        // a divide-by-zero ratio in that brief window.
        const hasDuration = scDurationMs > 0;
        const ratio = hasDuration ? Math.min(1, Math.max(0, scPositionMs / scDurationMs)) : 0;
        if (timelineFill instanceof HTMLElement) timelineFill.style.width = ratio * 100 + '%';
        if (timelineThumb instanceof HTMLElement) timelineThumb.style.left = ratio * 100 + '%';
        if (timeCurrentEl) timeCurrentEl.textContent = formatTime(scPositionMs / 1000);
        if (timeDurationEl) timeDurationEl.textContent = hasDuration ? formatTime(scDurationMs / 1000) : '0:00';
      }

      function seekFromClientX(clientX) {
        if (!scWidget || !scReady || !(timelineTrack instanceof HTMLElement) || scDurationMs <= 0) return;
        const rect = timelineTrack.getBoundingClientRect();
        const ratio = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, (clientX - rect.left) / rect.width));
        scPositionMs = ratio * scDurationMs;
        scWidget.seekTo(scPositionMs);
        updateTimelineUI();
      }

      // Window-level move/up only ever need binding once — window itself
      // is never replaced by a navigation, and both read timelineTrack /
      // seekingTimeline live via closure, so they stay correct once those
      // are reassigned by bindOverlayInteractions on a later navigation.
      // The pointerdown that actually STARTS a drag lives on timelineTrack
      // itself though, and that element IS replaced every navigation — see
      // bindOverlayInteractions below for where it gets rebound.
      window.addEventListener('pointermove', (e) => {
        if (!seekingTimeline) return;
        seekFromClientX(e.clientX);
      });
      window.addEventListener('pointerup', () => {
        seekingTimeline = false;
      });

      // No timeupdate-equivalent listener setup here anymore — SC.Widget's
      // PLAY_PROGRESS event (bound in initSoundCloudWidget below) drives
      // scPositionMs/updateTimelineUI() instead, since there's no <audio>
      // element to attach a native event to.
      updateTimelineUI();

      // Volume — mute toggle + a native range input restyled to match (see
      // the --vol custom property in <style> above). Big-overlay-only, same
      // as the timeline. scVolume/scMuted are the source of truth (declared
      // up top) rather than reading back from scWidget — like play/pause,
      // its setVolume() call is fire-and-forget with no synchronous
      // property to read.
      function updateVolumeUI() {
        if (volumeSlider instanceof HTMLInputElement) {
          volumeSlider.value = String(scVolume);
          volumeSlider.style.setProperty('--vol', String(scMuted ? 0 : scVolume));
        }
        muteBtn?.setAttribute('data-muted', scMuted || scVolume === 0 ? 'true' : 'false');
      }

      // Touch fallback for the volume popover — see its :hover/:focus-within
      // CSS rule's own comment above for why touch needs one at all. Tapping
      // the mute button already toggles mute via the listener above (this
      // bubbles from the same tap); toggling .is-open alongside that is a
      // deliberate combined action on touch — tap once to mute AND reveal
      // the slider for further adjustment — rather than needing a second,
      // separate tap target purely to reveal it. Doesn't interfere with
      // desktop's hover reveal: this is an OR, not a replacement (see the
      // CSS comment) — clicking the mute button on desktop toggling this
      // class too is harmless since hover already independently shows/hides
      // the same popover regardless of the class's state. Bound once on
      // `document` (stable) rather than per-navigation — reads the current
      // `volumeControl` live via closure, so it stays correct once
      // bindOverlayInteractions reassigns it.
      document.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
        if (!volumeControl || !volumeControl.classList.contains('is-open')) return;
        if (e.target instanceof Element && volumeControl.contains(e.target)) return;
        volumeControl.classList.remove('is-open');
      });

      // Escape closes the overlay regardless of focus — bound once on
      // `document` for the same reason as the click-outside listener above;
      // closeOverlay() itself reads the current `overlay` live via closure.
      document.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
        if (e.key === 'Escape') closeOverlay();
      });

      // Re-queries and re-binds every #music-overlay-scoped element (see the
      // `let overlay, overlayArtImg, ...` declaration up top for the full
      // list and why they're `let`) and repaints the overlay's current
      // state onto whatever fresh copy Astro just rendered. Called once
      // here for the very first load, and again on every astro:page-load —
      // this IS the fix for "music player pe bhi click nahi hota album
      // wala until reload": every button below was previously bound once,
      // ever, to the very first #music-overlay; any navigation after that
      // silently left them pointing at a detached node.
      function bindOverlayInteractions() {
        overlay = document.getElementById('music-overlay');
        if (!overlay || overlay.dataset.owBound === 'true') return;
        // Guards against binding the SAME live element twice — confirmed
        // live: astro:page-load fires an extra time right after the very
        // first page load's own top-level call (same double-invocation
        // Astro's lifecycle produces elsewhere, see index.astro's
        // typewriteHeadline comment), and since nothing navigated away
        // between those two firings, both calls queried this identical
        // #music-overlay and each attached its own click listener to
        // queueBtn/overlayPlayBtn/volumeControl/etc. A click then fired
        // BOTH listeners — for anything toggle-based (queue panel open/
        // closed, togglePlay's play/pause) that's toggle-then-toggle-back
        // in the same event, netting zero: "the queue button doesn't do
        // anything." A fresh #music-overlay from a real navigation has no
        // dataset yet, so this only skips the redundant SAME-element
        // re-bind, never a genuine rebind after navigating.
        overlay.dataset.owBound = 'true';
        overlayArtImg = document.getElementById('music-overlay-art-img');
        overlayTitleEl = document.getElementById('music-overlay-title');
        overlayArtistEl = document.getElementById('music-overlay-artist');
        overlayPlayBtn = document.getElementById('music-overlay-play');
        overlayPrevBtn = document.getElementById('music-overlay-prev');
        overlayNextBtn = document.getElementById('music-overlay-next');
        queuePanel = document.getElementById('music-overlay-queue-panel');
        timelineTrack = document.getElementById('music-overlay-timeline-track');
        timelineFill = document.getElementById('music-overlay-timeline-fill');
        timelineThumb = document.getElementById('music-overlay-timeline-thumb');
        timeCurrentEl = document.getElementById('music-overlay-time-current');
        timeDurationEl = document.getElementById('music-overlay-time-duration');
        muteBtn = document.getElementById('music-overlay-mute');
        volumeSlider = document.getElementById('music-overlay-volume-slider');
        volumeControl = document.getElementById('music-overlay-volume-control');
        queueBtn = document.getElementById('music-overlay-queue');

        overlayPlayBtn?.addEventListener('click', togglePlay);
        overlayPrevBtn?.addEventListener('click', () => {
          loadTrack((currentTrackIndex - 1 + TRACKS.length) % TRACKS.length, { autoplay: scPlayingState });
        });
        overlayNextBtn?.addEventListener('click', () => {
          loadTrack((currentTrackIndex + 1) % TRACKS.length, { autoplay: scPlayingState });
        });

        timelineTrack?.addEventListener('pointerdown', (e) => {
          seekingTimeline = true;
          seekFromClientX(e.clientX);
        });

        muteBtn?.addEventListener('click', () => {
          scMuted = !scMuted;
          if (scWidget && scReady) scWidget.setVolume(scMuted ? 0 : scVolume * 100);
          updateVolumeUI();
        });
        volumeSlider?.addEventListener('input', () => {
          if (!(volumeSlider instanceof HTMLInputElement)) return;
          scVolume = parseFloat(volumeSlider.value);
          scMuted = false;
          if (scWidget && scReady) scWidget.setVolume(scVolume * 100);
          updateVolumeUI();
        });
        volumeControl?.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
          // Dragging the slider itself fires 'click' too on release — don't
          // let that close a popover the user is actively adjusting.
          if (e.target instanceof Element && e.target.closest('.music-overlay-volume-slider')) return;
          volumeControl.classList.toggle('is-open');
        });

        queueBtn?.addEventListener('click', () => {
          const open = queuePanel?.classList.toggle('is-open');
          queueBtn.setAttribute('aria-expanded', open ? 'true' : 'false');
        });
        // Clicking a queue item jumps to that track — delegated on the
        // panel itself rather than per-item listeners, since renderQueue
        // rebuilds the items (and would otherwise need to reattach a
        // listener on every rebuild).
        queuePanel?.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
          const item = e.target instanceof Element ? e.target.closest('.music-overlay-queue-item') : null;
          if (!(item instanceof HTMLElement) || !item.dataset.trackIndex) return;
          const index = Number(item.dataset.trackIndex);
          if (index === currentTrackIndex) return;
          loadTrack(index, { autoplay: scPlayingState });
        });

        overlay?.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
          if (e.target === overlay) closeOverlay();
        });

        // Repaint the fresh elements with the CURRENT state — a newly
        // rendered #music-overlay starts with its hardcoded default HTML
        // (first track's title/art, volume slider at its default value),
        // which won't match reality if e.g. the user had already switched
        // tracks or muted before navigating.
        updateNowPlayingUI();
        updateTimelineUI();
        updateVolumeUI();
      }
      bindOverlayInteractions();
      document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', bindOverlayInteractions);

      // Wires up the actual SoundCloud player. SC (the global the
      // w.soundcloud.com/player/api.js script attaches) should already be
      // defined by the time this runs — that <script> tag sits earlier in
      // <body> with no async/defer, so the browser blocks parsing until
      // it's fetched and executed, same ordering guarantee normal
      // synchronous scripts always get. Guarded with a typeof check anyway
      // rather than assumed, since a network hiccup on that external script
      // shouldn't throw and break the rest of this init block.
      function initSoundCloudWidget() {
        if (!(scIframe instanceof HTMLIFrameElement) || typeof SC === 'undefined') return;
        scWidget = SC.Widget(scIframe);

        scWidget.bind(SC.Widget.Events.READY, () => {
          scReady = true;
          scWidget.getDuration((ms) => {
            scDurationMs = ms;
            updateTimelineUI();
          });
          scWidget.setVolume(scMuted ? 0 : scVolume * 100);
        });

        // Authoritative for "is playing" — e.g. the track ending naturally
        // (FINISH), or being paused some way other than this site's own
        // button (SoundCloud's own embed chrome, if it were visible; media
        // keys). Redundant with togglePlay()'s own optimistic setPlaying()
        // call for a click specifically, but harmless: same value, set
        // twice.
        scWidget.bind(SC.Widget.Events.PLAY, () => {
          scPlayingState = true;
          setPlaying(true);
        });
        scWidget.bind(SC.Widget.Events.PAUSE, () => {
          scPlayingState = false;
          setPlaying(false);
        });
        scWidget.bind(SC.Widget.Events.FINISH, () => {
          scPlayingState = false;
          setPlaying(false);
        });

        // Fires roughly every 250ms while playing — the closest thing to
        // <audio>'s old 'timeupdate' for this engine.
        scWidget.bind(SC.Widget.Events.PLAY_PROGRESS, (e) => {
          scPositionMs = e.currentPosition;
          updateTimelineUI();
        });
      }
      initSoundCloudWidget();

      document.addEventListener('astro:before-preparation', closeOverlay);
      document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', refreshRouteVisibility);
      refreshRouteVisibility();
      refreshIcon();

      // First-open/reload discoverability hint, Home only: nudge the
      // widget out on its own for a few seconds so a new visitor notices
      // it's there and interactive, then let it slide back — direct
      // instruction ("should come out of its way to show the play button
      // state for few seconds and go back in"). Deliberately placed inside
      // this one-time init block (guarded by __musicWidgetInit above)
      // rather than on every 'astro:page-load' — that guard only re-runs on
      // an actual hard page load/reload (a fresh script execution), so
      // arriving at Home via an ordinary SPA nav from another page (e.g.
      // the dock's Home icon) does NOT replay this, matching "fresh load...
      // first time open or reload" rather than every visit to Home.
      // setExpanded(true) also marks everHovered, so the icon reads as a
      // real play/pause status (not the chevron) both during this peek and
      // on every real hover afterward — the peek doubles as teaching what
      // the icon means.
      if (location.pathname === '/') {
        setTimeout(() => {
          setExpanded(true);
          setTimeout(() => setExpanded(false), 2500);
        }, 800);
      }
    }
  </script>

  <!-- Sitewide autoplay-recovery fix, independent of the music widget above
       (own script, own concern, nothing shared). Root cause, confirmed live
       via the browser's own console (not guesswork): a <video autoplay>
       whose load was attempted while its container was still display:none
       (any tabbed/paneled section that isn't the default-visible one) can
       get stuck in a failed resource-selection state — networkState
       NETWORK_NO_SOURCE, video.error set, and critically, calling .play()
       on it afterward does NOT retry the load; it just rejects forever with
       "NotSupportedError: no supported sources". Direct report: "page
       reload krne pe chal ja rahe" (works on a hard reload — the video
       loads only after loading, resource selection succeeds fine — but not
       after switching to it in-app). .load() explicitly restarts resource
       selection; only called when actually stuck, never on an
       already-playing video, or it'd restart it from 0 every time this
       runs. This is the sitewide safety net; Writings' Captures tab and the
       Photoshop 2030 case study have their own copies of the same fix
       scoped to their own panels, for the exact moment their tab becomes
       visible — this one catches anything else. Runs after EVERY
       astro:page-load (not gated behind a run-once guard, unlike the music
       widget's own init above) since it must re-arm for each new page. -->
  <script>
    function playAutoplayVideos() {
      document.querySelectorAll('video[autoplay]').forEach((video) => {
        if (!(video instanceof HTMLVideoElement)) return;
        if (video.error || video.networkState === HTMLMediaElement.NETWORK_NO_SOURCE) {
          video.load();
        }
        if (video.paused) {
          video.play().catch(() => {});
        }
      });
    }
    playAutoplayVideos();
    document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', playAutoplayVideos);
  </script>
</body>
</html>
